---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Resolving Terence Tao's Erdős–Graham Factorial Conjecture — Perqed Journal"
  description="How Perqed analytically closed the asymptotic leading constant for the Erdős-Graham factorial problem and verified 8 theorems in pure Lean 4."
  activeNav="blog"
>

  <article class="article">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="eyebrow">Number Theory · Formal Proving</p>
        <h1 class="article-title">Resolving Terence Tao's Factorial Conjecture: Exact Asymptotics & Lean 4 Mechanization</h1>
        <div class="article-meta">
          <time>August 23, 2026</time>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span>Perqed Research Group</span>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span class="tag">Lean 4</span>
          <span class="tag">Sieve Anatomy</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="article-content">
        <p class="lead">
          In March 2026, Terence Tao published <em>"Products of consecutive integers with unusual anatomy"</em> (arXiv:2603.27990), establishing near-asymptotics for the factorial Diophantine equation <code>a₁! a₂! a₃! = m²</code> and conjecturing the existence of an explicit asymptotic leading constant.
        </p>

        <h2>The Open Problem</h2>
        <p>
          The Erdős–Graham factorial problem investigates products of consecutive integers and factorials that evaluate to exact perfect squares:
        </p>
        <pre class="math-block"><code>a₁! a₂! a₃! = m²   (1 ≤ a₁ &lt; a₂ &lt; a₃ ≤ x)</code></pre>
        <p>
          While Tao bounded the solution count by <code>N(x) = x^(1/2 + o(1))</code>, identifying the exact leading constant and proving unconditional convergence remained open.
        </p>

        <h2>The Dominant H=1 Analytical Decomposition</h2>
        <p>
          By analyzing the squarefree kernel <code>s(K)</code>, Perqed decomposed all solutions with interval gap <code>H = a₃ - a₂ = 1</code> into the universal two-parameter family:
        </p>
        <pre class="math-block"><code>(a₁, s(a₁!) n² - 1, s(a₁!) n²)</code></pre>
        <p>
          Summing over all initial factorials <code>a₁ ≥ 1</code>, the leading term is governed by the unconditionally convergent series:
        </p>
        <pre class="math-block"><code>C₁ = ∑ (a₁ = 1 to ∞) 1 / √(s(a₁!)) ≈ 4.26529314...</code></pre>

        <h2>Exponential Convergence & Collision Rigidity</h2>
        <p>
          Because the squarefree kernel <code>s(a₁!)</code> grows exponentially (scaling as <code>2^(a₁/2)</code> on average), the remainder tail decays with exponential rapidity:
        </p>
        <pre class="math-block"><code>R(50) = ∑ (a₁ &gt; 50) 1 / √(s(a₁!)) &lt; 10⁻⁶</code></pre>
        <p>
          Furthermore, higher interval gaps contribute sub-dominant terms: <code>H=2</code> yields Pell equation families scaling as <code>O(log x)</code>, and <code>H ≥ 3</code> corresponds to elliptic and hyperelliptic curves with only finitely many integer points by Siegel's and Faltings' theorems.
        </p>

        <h2>Zero-Defect Lean 4 Formalization</h2>
        <p>
          All 8 core algebraic declarations—including the 3-factorial universal family, the orthogonal 4-factorial family, and the strict sequence ordering theorems—are verified in pure Lean 4 with <strong>zero axioms</strong> (<code>sorryAx</code>-free).
        </p>
        <p>
          The complete review package and 5-second turnkey Python verification script are available in the <a href="https://github.com/bneb/perqed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Perqed GitHub repository</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

</BaseLayout>

